A brick can handle its own form: a contact form, a newsletter sign-up, a quote request. Give it an `action`, render a `<ActionForm>` in its component, and read the outcome from `props.submission`.

- **Without JavaScript** (Mortar's [pages without JavaScript](/docs/no-javascript)), the form is a plain post. The action runs on the server, and the browser comes back to the same page with the result, which the brick shows. A refresh doesn't post twice.
- **With JavaScript** (the admin, or a brick that hydrates), the same form is sent in the background, and only that brick updates. The page doesn't reload.

The brick is the same either way. The example site (`examples/site`) has a working contact form at `/contact`.

## The brick

```tsx
import { ActionForm, defineBrick, fields } from '@mortar-kit/core';
import { Show } from 'solid-js';

type Result = { status: 'sent' } | { status: 'invalid'; values: Record<string, string> } | { status: 'failed' };

export const contactForm = defineBrick({
  name: 'contact-form',
  label: 'Contact form',
  schema: {
    heading: fields.text({ label: 'Heading', default: 'Get in touch' }),
    to: fields.text({ label: 'Send to', help: "Empty: the site's inbox." }),
  },
  async action({ form, data, context }): Promise<Result> {
    const name = String(form.get('name') ?? '').trim();
    const email = String(form.get('email') ?? '').trim();
    const message = String(form.get('message') ?? '').trim();
    if (!name || !email.includes('@') || !message) return { status: 'invalid', values: { name, email, message } };
    const to = data.to || context.inbox;
    if (!context.mailer || !to) return { status: 'failed' };
    await context.mailer.send({ to, replyTo: email, subject: `Contact form: ${name}`, text: message });
    return { status: 'sent' };
  },
  component: (props) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{props.data.heading}</h2>
      <Show when={props.submission?.result?.status !== 'sent'} fallback={<p role="status">Thanks! We'll reply by email.</p>}>
        <ActionForm>
          <input name="name" required />
          <input name="email" type="email" required />
          <textarea name="message" required />
          <button type="submit" disabled={props.submission?.pending}>Send</button>
        </ActionForm>
      </Show>
    </section>
  ),
});
```

- `action` gets the posted `form` (untrusted: check every value), the entry's `data`, the `request`, and the app's `context`. What it returns is the result: keep it plain JSON, and small, since without JavaScript it rides back in a cookie (about 4 kB).
- `props.submission` is the entry's latest post: `pending`, then `result`, or `error` if the action threw. It's undefined until the form is sent.
- Write `action` before `component`, so the result has its type.

## Server-only services

The brick's module also reaches the browser (in the admin, and in a brick that hydrates), so an action doesn't import the mailer, a database or a secret. The app passes them in `context`, and declares their types by augmenting `ActionContext`:

```ts
declare module '@mortar-kit/core' {
  interface ActionContext {
    mailer?: Mailer;
    inbox?: string;
  }
}
```

## Wiring it up

Actions run through one server function in the app, which calls `content.runAction` with the reference and form the browser sent. That's the place for a per-client limit, since the form is public:

```ts
// src/server/content.ts
'use server';
export async function runBrickAction(ref: EntryRef, form: FormData | URLSearchParams): Promise<unknown> {
  const request = getRequestEvent()?.request;
  if (overLimit(request)) throw new Error('Too many form posts.');
  return respond(await content.runAction(ref, form, { request, context: { mailer, inbox } }), { revalidate: '' });
}
```

Then the app tells Mortar where forms post and how to read results back, with `BrickActionsContext`. With Solid's router, a form action over that server function does both. It posts plainly without JavaScript (the result comes back in a one-time cookie the router reads), and in the background with it:

```ts
// src/actions.ts
const post = formAction(runBrickAction, 'mortar-brick-action');

export const brickActions: BrickActions = {
  action: (ref) => post.with(ref),
  useSubmission: (ref) => {
    const all = useSubmissions(post, (input) => sameEntry(input[0], ref()));
    return () => {
      const last = all.at(-1);
      return last && { pending: last.result === undefined && last.error === undefined, result: last.result, error: last.error };
    };
  },
};
```

```tsx
// App.tsx: around the router
<BrickActionsContext value={brickActions}>
  <Router>…</Router>
</BrickActionsContext>
```

Finally, pass the document to `PageView`, so each form can name its entry: `<PageView bricks={bricks} entries={page.entries ?? []} doc={{ collection: 'pages', id: page.id }} />`. The admin preview does this itself. It doesn't send forms, so editors can click around in them.
